// Lore for points on the warhammer-40k maps. See src/data/lore/index.js.
export const WARHAMMER_40K_LORE = {
  // Segmentum Solar
  'terra-40k': 'The Throneworld, buried under ten thousand years of palace and archive, where the Emperor sits immobile on the Golden Throne while a billion functionaries push the paperwork of an empire that never stops dying. The Adeptus Terra rules from here by inertia as much as decree, and the world has not seen open war since the Heresy. To hold Terra is to hold the Imperium’s signature on every order it has ever given.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'eye-of-terror': 'A wound in reality more than a place, the Eye has swallowed whole battlefleets and spat out worlds that no longer obey the laws that made them. Chaos warbands muster in its bloody light before every Black Crusade, and the Imperium’s entire eastern strategy is built around making sure they never fully leave.',
  cadia: 'The fortress-world at the Cadian Gate, the one stable passage out of the Eye of Terror, garrisoned for ten thousand years by a population bred and drilled for nothing else. Cadia broke apart in the opening days of the thirteenth Black Crusade, and the Imperium has been fighting over its wreckage and the Gate behind it ever since, because whoever commands that chokepoint decides what comes out of the Eye and what does not.',
  'belis-corona': 'An armoury world within reach of the Cadian Gate, its manufactora casting the guns and hulls that keep the Imperial Navy in the field along the Obscurus front. Every Black Crusade that spills out of the Eye tests Belis Corona early, because a fleet that cannot resupply here has to run all the way back to Cypra Mundi.',
  agripinaa: 'A forge world whose manufactora turn out little but engines of war — tank hulls, gun platforms, the heavy iron that keeps the Guard armies of the Obscurus front supplied. It answers to Mars in name more than deed, and its Fabricator-Locum weighs every requisition against how much the world can still spare after a century of total war.',
  hydraphur: 'The naval bastion that anchors the Obscurus fleets, its orbital docks old enough to have serviced ships since before the Heresy. An Adeptus Arbites precinct-fortress rides in high orbit as judge over the system, and between the two of them Hydraphur decides who is allowed to sail further in.',
  'fenris-40k': 'A world of ice and wolves, home to the Space Wolves and the tribes they still recruit from, more feral than most Imperial holdings and prouder of it for the fact. The Chapter’s Fang broods over the system like a mountain that answers back, and no sane raider tests a homeworld with that many sons still living on it.',
  armageddon: 'A hive and manufacturing world worked to exhaustion long before Ghazghkull Thraka first came for it, its factories turning out weapons and its ash wastes turning into battlefields on a schedule the locals could set their calendars by. Three wars for Armageddon have already been fought here, and the Orks keep coming back because the place is worth having, not because they enjoy losing.',
  'the-rock': 'An asteroid the size of a small moon, hollowed into a fortress-monastery and crewed by the Dark Angels for ten thousand years, said to be a shard of the Chapter’s lost homeworld Caliban. It goes where the Inner Circle’s secrets need guarding, which in practice means wherever the Fallen might be found.',
  'prospero-40k': 'The dead world of Prospero, burned to slag by the Space Wolves during the Heresy and never resettled, its libraries and its people gone with it. The Thousand Sons who survive as Rubric Marines still call it home in whatever way ash can be a home, and the warp around it has never quite settled back down.',

  // Segmentum Pacificus
  vordrast: 'A system watched over by an Adeptus Custodes star-keep, close enough to the Eye’s outriders that haemonculi covens have tried to fortify its gas giants more than once. The Custodes do not garrison worlds lightly, and Vordrast’s neighbours have learned that anything built here without leave gets crushed rather than negotiated with.',
  macharia: 'A shrine world of Segmentum Pacificus, once called Donia before it took the name of the man born there, Lord Commander Solar Macharius, whose crusade from this world conquered a thousand systems in seven years. Pilgrims and recruiting officers arrive in equal numbers, one hoping for a miracle and the other hoping to raise the next Macharius.',
  chiros: 'An agri world of forests and lakes far richer than its handful of million inhabitants need, its exports running to rejuvenating elixirs, fine furs and narcotics distilled from things that grow nowhere else. Rogue traders and Administratum auditors alike find reasons to linger, since Chiros pays better than most worlds its size ever should.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  krieg: 'A world still poisoned by the atomic war it fought against its own rebel governor ten thousand years ago, its surface a wasteland of craters and its people bred since to atone for it in the trenches. The Death Korps regiments raised here are spent by their own commanders like ammunition, because Krieg has never run short of volunteers who believe they owe the Emperor a life.',
  gathalamor: 'A martyr’s world, holding the tomb of Confessor Dolan Chirosius, whose death here at a corrupt cardinal’s order turned Gathalamor into an uprising and then a shrine. It went dark with half the Imperium during the Noctis Aeterna and was fought back into the light by the Indomitus Crusade, which is reason enough for every Ecclesiarchy faction to want a relic of the place.',
  'necromunda-40k': 'A hive world stacked a hundred levels deep, its spires ruled by noble houses and its underhive by gangs, mutants and things worse than either. Necromunda exports enough promethium and raw ore to matter to the wider Imperium, but most of what happens there stays exactly where it started — one level down.',

  // Segmentum Tempestus
  ophelia: 'A garden world of manicured estates and old aristocracy, whose cadet schools have supplied Imperial Guard officers to a dozen sectors for centuries. Ophelia trades on bloodline and bearing as much as competence, and somehow keeps producing commanders good enough to justify both.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  'tallarn-40k': 'A desert world whose population still lives half underground, a habit learned during the Iron Warriors’ chemical bombardment in the Heresy and never quite unlearned. The Tallarn regiments raised here fight from light vehicles at a gallop, and no Administratum requisition officer has ever dared ask why they never seem to run out of fuel.',
  catachan: 'A jungle deathworld where the wildlife is more dangerous than most invading armies, which is why the Guard regiments raised here are considered elite the moment they are old enough to enlist. Catachan exports soldiers, not resources, and the Munitorum has never once complained about the trade.',

  // Imperium Nihilus
  golgotha: 'An Ork world by the time anyone in the Imperium thought to contest it, seized and infested by Ghazghkull Thraka’s forces after the second war for Armageddon. A purge sent to burn it out and kill the warlord failed outright, and Golgotha has sat ever since as proof that some worlds are simply lost until an army large enough to retake them can be spared.',
  'lastrati-40k': 'One of the Nine Hollow Worlds, a system of habitable interiors wrapped in the impassable Siren Clouds, purged by the Imperium more than once for the corruptions its isolation lets fester. The Red Corsairs have made a project of it in recent centuries, and every Munitorum planner knows Lastrati as a name that reappears on crusade rosters every few centuries whether anyone wants it to or not.',
  maelstrom: 'A permanent warp storm sprawled across a stretch of Imperium Nihilus, its edges thick with pirate kingdoms, renegades and worlds no Administratum census has updated in centuries. Nothing sane sails into the Maelstrom itself, but everything worth having in the sector around it washes up on the storm’s shores sooner or later.',
  badab: 'The system that gave its name to the Badab War, when the Astral Claws and their allied Chapters broke from Imperial rule over tithes and territory and had to be put down by a fleet of loyalist Chapters in turn. What is left of Badab is a lesson the Adeptus Terra likes repeating: a Chapter with too much of its own territory eventually starts acting like it owns it.',

  // Segmentum Tempestus
  'nocturne-40k': 'A volcanic deathworld of firestorms and obsidian, homeworld of the Salamanders and the forges where every one of their weapons is still hand-cast in the old way. Nocturne trains its people to endure the Trial of Fire before it trains them to fight, and the Chapter it produces is unusually patient with everyone who is not trying to burn its world down.',
  'sirens-storm': 'A stretch of the Tempestus fleet lanes named for the warp static that drowns vox traffic for a dozen systems around it, useful chiefly to whoever wants a fight the rest of the segmentum will not hear about until it is over. Imperial convoys route around it when they can and fight through it when they cannot.',
  aleusis: 'A minor Imperial world along the Tempestus supply lanes, unremarkable except that its position makes it a waystation every fleet moving through the segmentum eventually has to use. Whoever garrisons Aleusis controls very little territory and a great deal of traffic.',
  solstice: 'A world named for the long stillness of its axial tilt, its single unbroken season making it a reliable agri-world in a segmentum where reliable is rare. Solstice feeds garrisons on three neighbouring fronts, which makes its harvest a strategic asset whether the farmers bringing it in think of it that way or not.',
  'gryphonne-iv': 'Once the Lost Forge, a manufactorum world whose defences rivalled Mars itself and whose Titan legion, the War Gryphons, marched to war beside its Skitarii. Hive Fleet Leviathan cut Gryphonne IV off from relief and scoured it clean within days, and what little now orbits the ash is fought over only by those hunting for wreckage worth the risk.',
  bakka: 'The Segmentum Tempestus naval headquarters, its shipyards laying keels for the Imperial Navy since the Great Crusade and its lava-sea surface mined for the ore that goes straight into new hulls. Battlefleet Bakka musters here before every major campaign in the segmentum, which makes the system itself a target no admiral can afford to lose.',
  'rynns-world': 'Homeworld of the Crimson Fists, a world the Chapter nearly lost outright when Warlord Snagrod’s Waaagh! broke its garrison and burned its monastery to the foundations. What remains is rebuilt smaller and harder than before, and the Crimson Fists have never forgiven Snagrod’s kind for making them prove it could be done.',
  antagonis: 'A frontier system on the Tempestus border, valued less for what it produces than for the early warning its listening posts give against anything moving in from further out. Antagonis has never fought a war of its own, only announced everyone else’s.',
  agrax: 'A mining world scoured to bedrock over centuries of extraction, its export manifests longer than its population registry. What Agrax lacks in people it makes up for in raw ore, and the Administratum has never let a depleted world go entirely while there is still something left to dig out of it.',
  'banes-landing': 'Named for the exploratory fleet that vanished making planetfall here and was found, generations later, entirely dead. Bane’s Landing was resettled anyway, because the alternative was leaving good ground empty, and its colonists keep the original site as consecrated ground.',
  'san-leor': 'A temperate world settled early in the Imperium’s long expansion and left largely to itself since, its chief virtue simply being unremarkable in a segmentum that reserves attention for whatever is currently on fire. San Leor’s governors prefer it that way and spend accordingly to keep it so.',
  balor: 'A system marked on Administratum charts mainly as a hazard, its primary star prone to flares violent enough to strip void shields from anything caught in orbit at the wrong hour. Fleets shelter in Balor’s outer dark rather than risk the inner system, and pirates have learned to time their raids by the same calendar.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'cirillo-prime': 'An agri-world feeding several of Ultima Segmentum’s hungrier hives, its plains given over entirely to grain and its skies to the bulk haulers that carry it away before the next harvest even ripens. Cirillo Prime has no army worth mentioning, which is exactly why it needs one.',
  schindelgheist: 'A cold, thinly populated world whose name is older than the current Imperial settlement on it, taken from a native superstition about voices in the wind that the first colonists never quite managed to disprove. Schindelgheist matters now chiefly for the listening station built to make sense of those same signals, in case they turn out to be worth worrying about.',
  charadon: 'The breadbasket and armoury of Ultima Segmentum, a name that covers a whole sector of mineral-rich worlds, gas giants and agri-worlds built to feed the forges further in. Typhus turned the sector’s heart to plague and rust in the War of Rust and Slime, and Charadon has been fighting to put itself back together, forge by forge, ever since.',
  'ichar-iv': 'A world remembered for the ambush the White Scars sprang here against the Alpha Legion, using the Legion’s own methods of deception against it and winning a rare, total victory over an enemy that almost never allows one. Ichar IV is quiet now, but every White Scars officer still studies the battle as proof that patience is a weapon too.',
  'macragge-40k': 'The Ultramarines’ homeworld and the seat of Roboute Guilliman’s Imperium Sanctus, its Fortress of Hera the closest thing the Imperium has to a second capital. Macragge has weathered Tyranid invasion and worse without falling, and the Chapter that holds it treats that record as a standard the rest of the Imperium is expected to live up to.',
  'black-reach': 'A warren of tunnels and wrecked hab-domes beneath a besieged forge world, packed with greenskins who have been breeding and building war machines in the dark for longer than the garrison above has been aware of them. Every attempt to seal Black Reach only slows what is coming, and the Orks down there show no sign of running out of room to grow.',
  salem: 'A world with a long and well-earned reputation for burning its own heretics before the Inquisition arrives to ask questions, which has saved outside investigators a great deal of paperwork over the centuries. Salem’s zeal is useful to the Imperium right up until it turns on someone it should not have, which local history suggests happens about once a generation.',
  leviathan: 'A stretch of dead systems marking the wake Hive Fleet Leviathan cut through Ultima Segmentum, stripped of biomass and largely of purpose since the swarm passed through. Salvage crews and archaeotech hunters pick through the wreckage anyway, on the theory that anything the tyranids left behind must not have been worth eating.',

  // Segmentum Pacificus
  commorragh: 'The webway city that serves the Drukhari as capital, a labyrinth without daylight where a thousand Kabals plot against each other as enthusiastically as they raid realspace. No outside fleet has ever conquered Commorragh, mostly because no outside fleet has ever found all of it.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'cypra-mundi': 'The administrative and fleet capital of Segmentum Obscurus, a forge world with a Segmentum Fortress in permanent orbit and enough shipyards to rebuild a battlefleet from the keel up. Cypra Mundi survived the Iron Warriors’ machine-curse during the Noctis Aeterna and has not let anyone forget it, since the world that runs a segmentum’s navy is not supposed to be the one under siege.',

  // Imperium Nihilus
  valhalla: 'An ice world whose people are bred to cold the way other worlds are bred to heat, supplying the Valhallan regiments that have fought on every kind of front despite being built for exactly one kind of weather. Valhalla’s tithe of soldiers has never once come up short, whatever else the segmentum has demanded of it.',
  'baal-40k': 'The Blood Angels’ irradiated homeworld, its deserts glassed by an ancient war and its people mutated into the feral tribes the Chapter still recruits from. Baal has been fought over by Tyranids and worse in recent memory, and the Blood Angels defend it with a fervour that owes as much to guilt over their primarch’s fate as to duty.',
  mephrit: 'Territory claimed by the Mephrit Dynasty, Necrons who specialised in detonating stars during the ancient War in Heaven and have not grown gentler with age. Their phaeron was lost in stasis and the dynasty now moves without a single guiding hand, which makes it no less dangerous and considerably harder to predict.',
  dimmamar: 'Birthplace of Sebastian Thor, who overthrew the tyrant Vandire and reformed the Ecclesiarchy, and site of the Confederation of Light he founded to do it. The Adepta Sororitas keep close watch on the world’s shrines, which proved wise when raiders out of Craftworld Ulthwé struck it and were driven off only by a Seraphim officer willing to hunt their farseer down personally.',
  naogeddon: 'An industrial world on the approaches to the Armageddon sector, its foundries kept running for whichever army needs resupply before the next push toward the more famous warzone next door. Naogeddon has never hosted a battle worth naming, and its foremen intend to keep it that way.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'new-drekport': 'A frontier port thrown up around a wrecked colony ship and never properly finished, its name a joke that stuck after the first one. New Drekport trades in salvage, rumour and whatever a ship’s captain would rather not declare at a more respectable dock.',
  'quisto-rol': 'A contested world on the frontier between Imperial and T’au space, claimed and reclaimed often enough that its charts carry survey stamps from both empires layered over each other. Quisto’rol produces little of value on its own; its worth is entirely in being a line someone else has to cross first.',
  'nexus-iii': 'A relay world whose orbital array routes astropathic and vox traffic across a wide stretch of Ultima Segmentum, more valuable as a listening post than as territory. Whoever controls Nexus III’s array hears a great deal that everyone else would rather they did not.',
  angelis: 'A shrine world built around a Living Saint’s final battlefield, its cathedral-fields tended by pilgrims who never quite outnumber the soldiers stationed to keep them safe. Angelis produces no wealth the Administratum can tax, only faith, which the Ecclesiarchy insists is worth just as much.',
  'somnium-stars': 'A cluster where ships’ crews report unusually vivid and unusually similar dreams for as long as they hold orbit, a phenomenon the Navigators blame on old warp scarring and the Inquisition has never bothered to disprove. The Somnium Stars are avoided more from superstition than evidence, which does not make the avoidance wrong.',
  formund: 'A garrison world holding one end of a supply corridor that several sectors depend on, its own resources modest but its position impossible to route around. Formund’s governors have made an entire local economy out of tolls and requisition fees.',
  'desperation-40k': 'A frontier world whose original name is lost to record and replaced, generations ago, by the one condition its garrison has never been without. Desperation sits astride a warp current that keeps dragging misfortune toward it, and every general posted here eventually stops asking for reinforcements and starts asking for evacuation orders instead.',
  hexos: 'A world under a standing Ordo Hereticus interdict, its population screened generation after generation for taint that never quite goes away. Hexos is watched more closely than it is governed, and the Inquisition has never explained why it simply does not burn the place down instead.',
  'kar-duniash': 'An old Guard-tithe world whose regiments still march under standards named for a Terran empire nobody living remembers founding. Kar Duniash sends soldiers rather than goods, and the Munitorum rates its discipline as worth more than most worlds’ entire tithe in materiel.',
  corinthe: 'A hive world saved from a converging tendril of Hive Fleet Moloch and the genestealer-infested hulk Bringer of Sorrow only by the timely arrival of the Dark Angels’ Deathwing, who fought the space hulk from the inside before the swarm could use it as a beacon. Corinthe’s grateful governors have kept a shrine to the Dark Angels ever since, which the Chapter finds more useful than they will admit.',
  vengeance: 'A world whose two founding colony fleets fell out before landfall and have fought a running vendetta across it ever since, long after either side remembers the original grievance. Imperial administrators have given up mediating and simply tax whichever faction currently holds the capital.',
  attila: 'A world of wide arid plains, homeworld of the Attilan Rough Riders who have ridden to war on it for as long as the Imperium has kept records. During the thirteenth Black Crusade its system became a battleground between Khornate daemons and the Necrons of the Sautekh Dynasty, which the locals point to as proof that Attila has always attracted violence it did not start.',
  'tau-empire': 'The expanding domain of the T’au, a young empire by Imperial reckoning that has already absorbed dozens of worlds through conquest, diplomacy and the sheer persuasive weight of the Greater Good. Every sept world it adds is another supply line the Imperium has to cut if the frontier is ever to be pushed back.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  'mars-40k': 'The Adeptus Mechanicus’ home and holiest forge, a red world of manufactora and data-temples where the Machine God is worshipped as devoutly as the Emperor is worshipped elsewhere. Mars supplies more war material than any other single world in the Imperium, which makes it the one forge world that can never be allowed to fall.',
  titan: 'A moon given over entirely to war: the forge-cathedrals where several Titan Legios build and bless their god-engines, and, in absolute secrecy, the fortress-monastery of the Grey Knights. Few outside either order know how much of Titan belongs to whom, and both would prefer to keep it that way.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'the-eternal-crusader': 'The Black Templars’ flagship, a Gloriana-class battleship gifted to Sigismund by Rogal Dorn and refitted so many times since that little of the original hull remains. Wherever the Eternal Crusader makes port, the High Marshal is aboard and a new crusade is about to be declared, so the ship’s current position matters more to the wider Imperium than most worlds ever will.',

  // The Eastern Fringe
  'watch-fortress-erioch': 'A Ramilles-class watch fortress holding vigil over the Jericho Reach, its armoured hull built atop the ruins of a xenos artefact older than Terra’s sun. The Deathwatch train and muster here between missions into the Reach’s endless wars, and the fortress’s own Omega Vault — sealed to every living hand that has tried it — is a mystery even its garrison has stopped trying to solve.',

  // Imperium Nihilus
  'storm-of-the-emperors-wrath': 'A crusade battlefleet holding station in Imperium Nihilus since the Great Rift split the galaxy, tasked with keeping open what supply lines still reach the worlds cut off from Holy Terra. The Storm of the Emperor’s Wrath does not so much occupy a system as haunt one, moving from crisis to crisis faster than reinforcements ever could.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  malfactus: 'A world quarantined after a Mechanicus experiment on it went far enough wrong that the Inquisition simply sealed the orbit and walked away. Malfactus is still there, still quarantined, and still occasionally sends up a signal that someone eventually has to go and investigate.',
  'craftworld-iyanden': 'A craftworld gutted by Hive Fleet Kraken, its population so reduced that the dead now outnumber the living and fight beside them as Wraithguard and Wraithlords. Iyanden’s survivors are unusually willing to ally with outsiders against the tyranids, having learned exactly what losing that fight costs.',

  // The Galactic Core
  'kin-holds': 'A loose scatter of independent human settlements at the galaxy’s crowded core, too far from Terra for the Administratum to tithe effectively and too stubborn to ask for Imperial protection even when it might help. The kin-holds trade with anyone, ally with no one for long, and have outlasted more than one empire that assumed they would be easy to absorb.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  mordian: 'A world shrouded permanently beneath a toxic upper atmosphere, its population living out their lives by artificial light and iron discipline. The Mordian Iron Guard regiments it raises fight in parade-ground order under real fire, and have never yet found a reason to stop.',
  piscina: 'An agri-world whose neighbouring moon, infested with Orks for millennia, finally erupted into the invasion that opened the third war for Armageddon. Piscina has been rebuilding ever since, in the uneasy knowledge that the moon overhead has done this before and could do it again.',
  chinchare: 'A minor world remembered locally for a governor’s dynasty that has held power for three hundred years through a mix of competence and convenient assassination. Chinchare’s politics are considered its own affair right up until the tithe comes due late, at which point they become everyone’s problem.',

  // Imperium Nihilus
  alaric: 'A garrison system anchoring one flank of the Nihilus defence line, its permanent fleet presence there to slow anything moving toward the more populous worlds behind it. Alaric has bought time for its neighbours more than once and rarely gets thanked for it.',
  molov: 'An industrial world on the projected path of a splinter fleet from the tyranid swarms further coreward, its planetary defence militia drilling for an invasion that has not arrived yet but is not expected to announce itself when it does. Molov’s foundries have not slowed production for a moment, which is either discipline or denial depending on who is asked.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  elysia: 'A world of floating cities and permanent storm-fronts below, home to the Elysian Drop Troops who are trained to arrive by grav-chute because their own world never gave them solid ground to march across. Elysia’s tithe arrives from the air on every world it is sent to, which the regiments consider a point of pride rather than a limitation.',
  ryza: 'A forge world unlike most others, its Mechanicus priesthood tolerant of open artistry and its plasma weapons prized across the Imperium for a reliability Mars has never quite matched. Ryza guards its manufacturing secrets as jealously as any world guards a relic, because in its case the secret is the relic.',
  'luther-mcintyre': 'An early Imperial colony named for the expedition commander who first planted a flag on it, back when that was still considered sufficient claim to a world. Luther McIntyre has grown into a modest garrison world since, its name outliving every detail of the man beyond the fact that he got there first.',

  // Segmentum Tempestus
  vrun: 'A mining concern more than a settled world, its population rotated in on fixed contracts and rotated out before local loyalties can take root. V’run answers to whichever Administratum office currently holds its extraction license, which changes more often than the miners would like.',
  illustris: 'A world of scholams and reliquaries, its libraries considered second only to Terra’s own for anything short of the most restricted texts. Illustris trades in knowledge rather than goods, and every faction from the Inquisition to rogue traders has reason to keep a foothold in its archives.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  perdus: 'The Perdus Rift, a region where the warp presses close enough to bend the rules of realspace, charted uneasily by both the Imperium and the T’au Empire’s nearby Sept of Dal’yth. Eldar raiders have used the rift as cover for generations, which was reason enough on its own for the Damocles Crusade to be launched into T’au space nearby.',
  'false-hope': 'A colony founded on a survey report that turned out to be wrong about almost everything except the world’s name, which the surviving settlers kept out of spite. False Hope has scraped by since on stubbornness and salvage, and considers outside help an insult more often than a mercy.',
  coelia: 'A temperate world whose orbital farms supply preserved rations to garrisons across half the segmentum, packed and shipped with an efficiency that owes more to fear of running short than any great affection for the work. Coelia rarely sees war directly, only the endless traffic of ships that live because of it.',
  asylus: 'A world set aside generations ago to take in refugees from worse fronts, and never quite emptied since because the fronts keep producing more of them. Asylus has long since run out of room, which its administrators consider a permanent, unsolvable problem rather than an emergency.',
  sable: 'A world mined for the black ore that gives it its name, valuable chiefly for the void-hulls it can be worked into once refined at Mars or Ryza. Sable ships out more raw material than it keeps, and its population has never seen the finished product their work becomes.',
  'sistec-prime': 'A minor forge world producing servitors and cogitation engines rather than weapons of war, its output feeding the Administratum’s endless appetite for record-keeping machinery. Sistec Prime is rarely fought over directly, but any campaign that drags on long enough eventually needs what it makes.',
  lunaphage: 'A system whose outer moons have been vanishing, one by one, for longer than local records go back, with no explanation anyone has confirmed and several nobody wants to. Lunaphage draws savants and lunatics in roughly equal numbers, and the Administratum has stopped trying to tell which is which before they ship out.',

  // Segmentum Pacificus
  'ultima-macharia': 'The furthest reach the Macharian Crusade ever recorded before its commander’s death ended the advance, a scatter of worlds brought into the Imperium in the crusade’s last, exhausted year. Ultima Macharia has been quietly compliant ever since, in the manner of a conquest nobody has had the resources to properly consolidate.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'hadex-anomaly': 'The Hadex Anomaly, a red hole of unknown origin at the heart of the Jericho Reach, said to spew time itself out of joint into the systems around it. Ghost ships drift frozen at its edges and the stars nearby are contaminated by its presence, and no serious power in the Reach claims to understand it, only to have learned to work around it.',
  'scourge-stars': 'A small empire of plague-worlds fallen to Nurgle north of Ultramar, ruled in the Daemon Primarch Mortarion’s name by Typhus and the Great Unclean Ones under him. The Ultramarines watch the Scourge Stars the way a healthy body watches an old wound, because it has never fully closed and shows no sign of trying to.',
  'necron-sautekh': 'Territory held by the Sautekh Dynasty, whose Phaeron Imotekh the Stormlord dragged his people from millennia of dormancy through will alone and has been expanding their reach ever since. The Sautekh do not so much reclaim worlds as reassert an ownership they never stopped believing was theirs.',
  'necron-nihilakh': 'Domain of the Nihilakh Dynasty, isolationist Necrons who venture out from their crownworld only when their considerable treasuries or their honour are threatened. Little is known of what the Nihilakh have hoarded across their long dormancy, and every tomb world under their Phaeron’s rule is assumed to be defended accordingly.',
  'ymga-monolith': 'An ancient structure of unmistakably Necron design, standing dormant long enough that three different empires have each, in turn, tried to claim the ground around it as their own. The Ymga Monolith has never activated for any of them, which everyone treats as reassuring right up until it stops being true.',
  calth: 'The world ambushed by the Word Bearers at the Heresy’s outset, its sky still scarred by the warp storm that swallowed half the planet’s surface in the opening massacre. Calth’s survivors dug in and never fully left, and the Ultramarines still work its mining tunnels as both a resource and a memorial to ten thousand dead.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  phalanx: 'The Imperial Fists’ fortress-monastery, a void-city of a ship old enough to predate the Imperium it now serves, armed and armoured beyond anything built since. The Phalanx moves only when Dorn’s sons judge a war worth the whole Chapter’s attention, which is rare enough that its arrival anywhere is itself a kind of warning.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  deliverance: 'A moon of choking industrial haze, adopted by the Raven Guard after their true homeworld was lost, its skies never clear enough to see the stars the Chapter is named for. Deliverance’s population has learned to live alongside a Chapter that prefers not to be seen arriving or leaving, which suits both parties fine.',
  chogoris: 'A world of endless steppe ruled by warring khanates, homeworld of the White Scars and the source of the Chapter’s horse-lord traditions and impatience with fixed fortifications. Chogoris measures strength the same way it always has, by how far and how fast a rider can carry it.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  medusa: 'A world of industrial wastes and toxic mining pits, homeworld of the Iron Hands and proving ground for the Chapter’s contempt for weak flesh. Medusa’s people replace what the world takes from them with augmetics as a matter of course, long before the Chapter ever comes to recruit.',

  // Imperium Nihilus
  cretacia: 'A feral world of prehistoric-scale wildlife, useful to the Imperium chiefly as a hardening ground for regiments that need to learn to fight something bigger than themselves before facing an enemy that shoots back. Cretacia has eaten more recruits than any war it has since been sent to, and the Munitorum considers that an acceptable rate of attrition.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'great-company-grimnar': 'The hunting ground claimed by Logan Grimnar’s Great Company, the Space Wolves’ Great Wolf and the Chapter’s supreme commander in all but formal title. Wherever Grimnar’s fleet currently makes port counts, for as long as it stays, as the most heavily defended stretch of the Fenris system’s holdings.',
  'great-company-blackmane': 'Territory held by Ragnar Blackmane’s Great Company, youngest of the Space Wolves’ Wolf Lords and, by the Chapter’s own reckoning, its most reliably reckless. Ragnar’s company takes the fights the older lords consider beneath careful planning, and wins often enough that nobody has talked him out of it yet.',
  'great-company-redmaw': 'The domain of Bran Redmaw’s Great Company, whose Wolf Lord is held by his own Chapter to walk closer to the wolf than the man. Redmaw’s warriors fight with a ferocity that unsettles even other Space Wolves, and his territory is left to him because no one is eager to argue the point.',
  'nemesis-tessera': 'An Imperial wasteland built over a secret Inquisitorial fortress, its dungeon levels holding daemons and worse under lock since long before most of the surface population knew the fortress was there. Nemesis Tessera has been stormed twice during Black Crusades, and both times whatever escaped was worse than the army that freed it.',
  sicarus: 'A daemon world within the Eye of Terror, claimed by the Word Bearers as a seat for their primarch Lorgar and given over entirely to cathedrals built in the Ruinous Powers’ honour. Sicarus has belonged to the Word Bearers for ten thousand years and shows every sign of belonging to them for ten thousand more.',
  medrengard: 'The Iron Warriors’ daemon world, a single continent-spanning fortification called the Eternal Fortress that the Legion has been building, breaking and rebuilding since the Heresy ended. Perturabo’s sons treat siege warfare as a religion here, and Medrengard is both their temple and their proof of doctrine.',

  // Segmentum Pacificus
  nostramo: 'A dead system now, its sun deliberately driven to nova by the Night Lords as they departed for the Heresy, so that the world which raised Konrad Curze would not survive to raise anyone else. The Night Lords who remain visit only to remember what was destroyed in their name, not to reclaim anything, because there is nothing left to reclaim.',
  eskrador: 'A haven for smugglers and unlicensed traders along the Pacificus reaches, tolerated by the Imperium chiefly because shutting it down would only scatter the same trade somewhere less convenient to watch. Eskrador’s economy runs on goods nobody files a manifest for.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'vesalius-reach': 'A stretch of contested space named for the mothballed medicae-research station at its heart, abandoned mid-project after funding and then the researchers vanished. Vesalius Reach draws scavengers hoping the old station’s records survived intact, and Inquisitorial patrols hoping they did not.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  gorkogrod: 'The capital the Ork warlord known as the Beast built on Ullanor Prime, centred on a temple raised over the exact ground where the Emperor and his Primarchs once stood in triumph. Vulkan fought his way into that temple to end the Beast personally, and Gorkogrod has been a byword since for how badly an old Imperial victory can curdle if left unattended.',
  redscar: 'An Ork world named, in the rough way such names stick, for the colour its northern wastes turn after every major scrap. Redscar produces nothing the Imperium wants except a reliable count of how many greenskins are currently spoiling for a fight in that stretch of space.',
  glitzgold: 'An Ork stronghold built up around the wreck of a captured treasure convoy, its warband more interested in looking impressive than in tactics, which has fooled exactly as many attackers as it has gotten killed. Glitzgold is rich by Ork standards and disorganised by anyone’s, a combination raiders find hard to resist testing.',
  bonegrinder: 'A world claimed by an Ork clan devoted to building ever larger crushing machines for reasons that make sense only to them. Bonegrinder’s greenskins measure status by the size of what they can flatten, and neighbouring systems have learned to stay well clear of the demonstrations.',

  // Segmentum Tempestus
  krumpton: 'An Ork settlement grown, gradually and without any plan, out of a crashed hulk’s scrapyard into something like a town. Krumpton’s greenskins fight each other as readily as outsiders, which has kept the place from ever quite organising into a real threat — so far.',
  scrapdrift: 'A debris field thick with wrecked hulks and the Orks who have made homes inside them, drifting slowly through the Tempestus shipping lanes on an orbit nobody has bothered to recalculate in decades. Scrapdrift is technically navigable and practically avoided, since no convoy captain wants to explain losing a ship to salvagers with guns.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'ophidian-gulf': 'A stretch of open void named for the coiling ion trails its resident raiders leave behind, hunted more than settled by anyone. The Ophidian Gulf belongs, in practice, to whichever raider fleet last won the argument over it, and the argument is never really finished.',

  // The Eastern Fringe
  'tendrils-end': 'The point on Administratum charts where a tyranid splinter fleet’s advance was finally, exhaustively halted, close enough to the Eastern Fringe that reinforcement took months to arrive at all. Tendril’s End is quiet now, but every world nearby keeps its defence grid live, on the theory that a name like that is not one to get comfortable around.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'gorgons-reach': 'A reach of space charted around the fossil remains of something vast and clearly not natural, predating every spacefaring race currently arguing over the salvage rights. Gorgon’s Reach has killed enough survey teams that most Imperial charters now list it as restricted rather than risk another one.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'craftworld-ulthwe': 'A craftworld that made its home deliberately in the shadow of the Eye of Terror, its Seer Council trading the safety of distance for centuries of warning about what the Eye might spill out next. Ulthwé’s people live under more foreseen doom than any other craftworld and have made peace with it, mostly by refusing anyone else the luxury of not helping.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'craftworld-biel-tan': 'The most militant of the craftworlds, its population devoted almost entirely to the Aspect Warrior shrines and to the dream of reclaiming worlds the old Eldar empire once held. Biel-Tan does not defend territory so much as reconquer it, and treats every human world in its path as a technicality to be corrected.',
  'craftworld-saim-hann': 'A craftworld of clans and Wild Riders who fight from jetbike and grav-tank at speeds the more cautious craftworlds consider reckless. Saim-Hann’s warriors ride to war the way their ancestors are said to have raced across their lost homeworld, and its Autarchs are chosen as much for daring as for wisdom.',
  meros: 'A world claimed in passing by more than one Eldar craftworld over the millennia, none willing to commit the resources to hold it outright. Meros changes hands quietly, in the Eldar fashion, long before any Imperial fleet nearby notices a fight has even happened.',

  // Segmentum Pacificus
  'craftworld-alaitoc': 'The most isolationist of the craftworlds, its population producing more Rangers and Path-walkers who leave the craftworld entirely than any other. Alaitoc watches the wider galaxy from a careful distance, and its scouts are often the first sign anyone else gets that the craftworld has taken an interest in their business at all.',
  'black-heart-enclave': 'An enclave held by the Kabal of the Black Heart, whose archon Asdrubael Vect has ruled Commorragh from the shadows for longer than most empires survive outright. The Black Heart’s reach extends into realspace far beyond this one enclave, but every thread of it answers, eventually, back here.',
  'poisoned-tongue-enclave': 'Territory of the Kabal of the Poisoned Tongue, a kabal that has survived Commorragh’s endless intrigue by preferring poison and rumour to open war whenever either will do the job. Rivals who underestimate how literally the kabal takes its own name rarely get a second chance to correct the mistake.',
  'flayed-skull-enclave': 'Holdings of the Kabal of the Flayed Skull, raiders whose reputation for flaying captives alive has made their name a threat other Dark Eldar invoke to keep slaves in line. The kabal’s enclave here serves as much as a trophy hall as a stronghold.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'tau-homeworld': 'The T’au homeworld and capital of the empire that takes its name, seat of the Ethereal caste and the philosophy of the Greater Good that binds every sept to it. Every T’au expansion, however far it reaches, is still explained back on this one world as being for the good of everyone it eventually conquers.',
  viorla: 'Vior’la, a sept world whose fire caste is famed even among the T’au for aggression bordering on recklessness, producing more decorated warriors than any sept but taking losses to match. The Greater Good is preached everywhere in the empire, but Vior’la is where it is preached loudest with a rifle in hand.',
  sacea: 'Sa’cea, one of the T’au Empire’s senior septs and a major supplier of fire warrior cadres and battlesuit crews to the wider war effort. Sa’cea’s forces are considered dependable rather than spectacular, which the T’au high command values more than either extreme.',
  borkan: 'Bork’an, the sept most devoted to the Earth Caste’s research, its laboratories responsible for a large share of the empire’s newest weapons and drone technology. Whatever the T’au field for the first time in a given war, there is a fair chance it left Bork’an’s foundries only months before.',

  // Segmentum Obscurus
  'kasr-kraf': 'The heart of Cadia’s kasr network and, by most accounts, the very last of the planet’s fortress-cities to hold when the thirteenth Black Crusade finally broke the world apart. What survives of Kasr Kraf’s garrison treats that distinction as both an honour and an unhealed wound, and neither sentiment shows signs of fading.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  'olympus-mons': 'The tallest peak on Mars, crowned with a forge-temple complex whose furnaces have run without pause since before the Imperium existed. Olympus Mons produces relics as often as weapons, and the difference between the two categories, on Mars, is often only a matter of paperwork.',
  graia: 'A forge world that spent generations under Necron siege, its population and Titan Legio fighting a war of attrition against tomb-worlds waking in its own system. Graia held, at ruinous cost, and the Mechanicus considers the world’s survival proof that even a forge cut off from Mars can win if it refuses to stop building weapons.',

  // Segmentum Tempestus
  'metalica-40k': 'The furnace-heart of the Charadon Sector’s forges, a world of foundries producing armour and weapons on an industrial scale even other forge worlds respect. Typhus and the Death Guard seized it during the war of rust and slime, and every ingot the Imperium has since reclaimed from Metalica has had to be fought back out of Nurgle’s grip first.',

  // Segmentum Solar
  'lucius-40k': 'A forge world whose surface has been fused to glass by weapons tested on it over millennia, dangerous to walk and beautiful to look at from orbit. Lucius produces some of the Mechanicus’ finest void shield technology, and its priesthood’s reputation for duelling arrogance is, by all accounts, entirely earned.',
  'stygies-viii': 'A forge world that builds its reputation on stealth rather than raw output, its Skitarii doctrines built around ambush and camouflage that most of the Mechanicus considers beneath the dignity of open war. Stygies VIII’s weapons rarely announce themselves before they fire, which has kept the world itself surprisingly hard to catch as well.',

  // Segmentum Tempestus
  'sacred-martyrium': 'A shrine built around the mass grave of a garrison that chose annihilation over surrender rather than lose the world to heresy. The Ecclesiarchy has never allowed the ground to be resettled, and pilgrims who visit the Sacred Martyrium are expected to leave having understood exactly what that choice cost.',
  'castellum-valorum': 'A fortress-world built purely to hold a strategic chokepoint, its population entirely military and its economy entirely logistics. Castellum Valorum has no history worth telling beyond the sieges it has survived, which its garrison considers the only history that matters.',
  'rose-fortress': 'A fortress-convent of the Adepta Sororitas, its outer walls grown thick with actual roses cultivated in memory of a Saint martyred nearby. The Rose Fortress garrison treats gardening and war as the same discipline, and visitors are advised not to ask which one they take more seriously.',
  'shrouded-reach': 'A stretch of space permanently dimmed by dust and gas thick enough to blind most augur arrays, useful chiefly to whoever wants to move a fleet without being noticed doing it. The Shrouded Reach has hidden ambushes for both sides of every war fought near it.',
  'ebon-sanctum': 'A monastery-world kept deliberately dark, its Chaplain-scholars holding that true devotion needs no light to see by. Ebon Sanctum trains some of the grimmer chaplains serving the Adeptus Astartes, and few outsiders stay long enough to learn why.',

  // Segmentum Pacificus
  'terryn-prime': 'A colony world named for the first captain to chart it, its economy built quietly around agriculture and asteroid mining ever since. Terryn Prime rarely makes the news the rest of the segmentum reads, which its population has always considered the ideal outcome.',
  'griffiths-landing': 'A coastal settlement grown up around the wreck of the survey vessel that first put people on the ground here, its harbour now busy with fishing fleets that have never quite forgotten the ship rusting at the bay’s mouth. Griffith’s Landing exports little the Imperium tracks closely, which suits everyone who lives there.',
  'ravens-roost': 'An outpost named for the black-winged scavengers that circle its highest crags, more numerous by far than the garrison stationed below them. Raven’s Roost holds a listening post rather than a population, watching a stretch of frontier that has been quiet for exactly as long as anyone can remember, which is precisely why the post has never been stood down.',
  'cadmus-primus': 'One of the older colonies in this stretch of Pacificus, its founding charter predating most of the current sector’s other Imperial claims. Cadmus Primus wears that seniority like a title, and newer worlds nearby resent it exactly as much as that implies.',

  // Ultima Segmentum
  'khymere-fane': 'A ruined temple-complex of a species no longer identifiable, resettled and reconsecrated by the Ecclesiarchy despite — or because of — the unease its old architecture still provokes. The Khymere Fane’s original purpose has never been established, and every attempt to excavate deeper has been quietly abandoned.',
  'lucaris-hold': 'A fortress-hold guarding a mineral-rich system on the Ultima frontier, garrisoned by rotating Guard regiments who rarely stay long enough to think of it as home. Lucaris Hold has changed hands in raids more than once, and each new garrison inherits the last one’s unfinished repairs along with the post.',

  // The Galactic Core
  'thurian-hold': 'A hold at the crowded galactic core, its name taken from an age of myth nobody now remembers the details of, only the grandeur. Thurian Hold survives at the core the way every hold there does: by being harder to take than it is worth taking.',
  'hyperian-hold': 'A fortified system named for a forgotten legend of towering strength, holding one of the busier trade junctions at the galaxy’s crowded heart. Hyperian Hold’s garrison spends more time policing traffic disputes than fighting outright wars, though the core has a way of turning the former into the latter without much warning.',
  'ymyr-hold': 'A cold, outlying hold at the edge of the Core’s crowded traffic, named for a giant out of half-remembered myth and defended with a stubbornness to match. Ymyr Hold’s worth lies less in what it produces than in the fact that losing it would open the approach to everything behind it.',
};
